# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# Undo takes back an edit, and a run of typing is ONE edit: a word goes
# in one step, not a letter at a time.  Redo puts it back.  Proven by
# reading the file after each: the whole word gone, then whole again.
wait 25
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Programming
wait 4
menu-pick TikuBasic
wait 150
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Open Project…
wait 40
click 400 220
wait 6
click 675 413
wait 60
click-window Project
wait 8
key Down
wait 8
key Return
wait 90
expect-title main.bas
note a word typed at the head of a line, and saved
key Home
wait 3
key x
key y
key z
wait 6
click 205 268
wait 60
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_home/main.bas xyz30 gamma
note ONE undo takes the WHOLE word -- the letters were folded into a
note single edit, so nothing of xyz is left, not even the xy a
note letter-at-a-time undo would leave behind
key z ctrl
wait 8
click 205 268
wait 60
expect-file-lacks /tmp/trk_home/main.bas xy
note and redo puts the whole word back
key z ctrl shift
wait 8
click 205 268
wait 60
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_home/main.bas xyz30 gamma
note the Edit menu names Undo and Redo, before the clipboard rows
click 117 8
wait 8
expect-menu Undo|Redo|Cut|Copy|Paste|Select all|
quit
